    "q-72342": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72342",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane.",
      "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{4-4y}{\\sqrt{5}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{4-4y}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(1-y)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5} & =z+4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y-1)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(1,-4).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-72342-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$(1,4)$ in the $yz$-plane",
